//? Title: SSD multiscale predictions and hard-negative mining
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Dense one-shot detectors produce many more background boxes than objects. This report verifies SSD default geometry, learned channel rescaling and tensor hard-negative mining, checks the full initialized SSD300 forward/backward graph, then visibly fits the complete compact scene model with Adam and evaluates its selected held-out detections.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.6_object_detection/ssd.cpp`.

> The method

SSD predicts several default shapes from each cell of six feature maps. Class-agnostic offsets refine those defaults, while per-image3:1 hard-negative mining balances the classification objective. Background is the last class in this source port. The full VGG16 SSD300 architecture is checked separately from the explicitly adapted64-pixel model that is fitted below.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/ssd_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/ssd_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/ssd_concept.tikz > build/ssd_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/ssd_concept.svg build/ssd_concept.dvi > build/ssd_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
