> Simple example: learned channel L2 rescaling

The first feature map is normalized per spatial location and channel-rescaled:
`y=x/sqrt(max(sum_channels(x²),1e-12))*gamma`. Full SSD300 initializes gamma to
20; the compact model uses 10. Gamma is a learned parameter, not running state.
A `[3,4]` channel vector has norm five and becomes `[12,16]` at scale 20.
Zero vectors remain zero and finite. Their input derivative is gamma divided by
`1e-6`, so finite zero output does not imply zero input derivative.
Only the first prediction head sees the rescaled map; the backbone continues
from the original unscaled activations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ssd_normalization.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::ParameterStore p(17, n::DType::Float64);
  const auto x =
      n::from_vector(std::vector<double>{3, 4, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 2, 2}).requires_grad_();
  const auto y = n::models::SsdNormalize(p, "fixture", x);
  if (y.to_vector<double>() != std::vector<double>{12, 16, 0, 0})
    return 1;
  const auto g = n::grad(n::sum(y), {x, p.at("fixture/scale")});
  if (std::abs(g[0].at({0, 0, 1, 0}) - 2e7) > 1e-8)
    return 1;
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.bars({-.18, .82, 1.82, 2.82}, x.to_vector<double>(), "input", {37, 99, 235}, .32)
      .bars({.18, 1.18, 2.18, 3.18}, y.to_vector<double>(), "rescaled", {22, 163, 74}, .32)
      .title("Learned L2 rescaling fixture")
      .x_label("flattened channel entries")
      .y_label("value")
      .x_limits(-.6, 3.6)
      .y_limits(0, 22);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.size(900, 500).save_svg("build/ssd_normalization.svg");
  std::print("[3,4,0,0] becomes[12,16,0,0]. Zero-vector input derivative{:.0f}; scale "
             "derivatives{:.6f}/{:.6f}. Gamma is fitted in the optimizer loop below.\n",
             g[0].at({0, 0, 1, 0}), g[1].at({0}), g[1].at({1}));
}
```
